I ran MaNGOS and ArcEmu servers ten years ago, and a World of Warcraft emulator
is still one of the better textbooks on distributed systems lying around in
public. Mostly because so little of it is distributed.

It is also the workload I know well enough to test a cluster honestly. Split it
up and almost all of it is boring, stateless plumbing that belongs in
[the k3s platform](/blog/k3s-platform-kit/). Exactly one process does not, and
that is the one that is the game.

These projects exist to study game server architecture and network protocol
design, and that is the only use I am discussing. If you run anything, the
legality of it is your own responsibility.

::: note
**Part 2 of 2 of [Where to start with
self-hosting](/blog/running-things-on-your-own-hardware/).** Previous: [K3s
starter kit on Hetzner cloud](/blog/k3s-platform-kit/)
:::

## The three pieces

The same three in every project I looked at: TrinityCore, AzerothCore, CMaNGOS,
MaNGOS and AscEmu. All of them are still alive, which surprised me.

- **An authentication daemon.** `authserver`, `realmd`, `logonserver`. It
  answers the login handshake and gives the client a list of realms.
- **A world daemon.** `worldserver`, `mangosd`. This one is the game: maps,
  creatures, spells, movement, loot.
- **A relational database**, split into auth, characters and world content.

The connection between the first two is a table, not an API. TrinityCore's auth
database has a `realmlist` row per realm with `address` and `port`
([realmlist](https://github.com/TrinityCore/tc-wiki/blob/main/database/335/auth/realmlist.md)),
documented as the world server's public address. The client logs in, gets that
address, and opens a raw TCP connection straight to the world daemon.

I had expected the client to talk to a front door that routes. It does not. **A
protocol that publishes its own endpoint has already made your topology decision
for you.** Whatever you put in front of it has to be a transparent TCP path, not
something you rewrite at an ingress.

The commercial version was a real distributed system. The CMaNGOS wiki sketches
separate machines for Login, Connection Manager, Realm, Map, a Battlegroup
server and several instance servers, connected by message queues
([Threading model](https://github.com/cmangos/issues/wiki/Threading-model)). The
open-source emulators fold Realm, Map and instances into one process, and that
single decision is the whole subject of this post.

## Why the world server is not a pod

TrinityCore's map manager walks every live map once per tick and either updates
them directly or hands them to a pool of workers, then waits for all of them:

{title="src/server/game/Maps/MapManager.cpp"}
```cpp
if (m_updater.activated())
    m_updater.schedule_update(*iter->second, uint32(i_timer.GetCurrent()));
else
    iter->second->Update(uint32(i_timer.GetCurrent()));
...
if (m_updater.activated())
    m_updater.wait();
```

And the unit of work a worker gets is one whole map. Two things follow from
that. Parallelism exists between maps and instances, never inside one. A
continent is a single `Map` object, and its update is one task on one thread.
And `m_updater.wait()` is a barrier on every single tick, so the tick costs what
the slowest individual map costs, no matter how many threads are sitting idle
behind it.

The shipped defaults say the same. TrinityCore ships `MapUpdate.Threads = 1`,
CMaNGOS ships 3, and both ship `Network.Threads = 1` with the note "Recommended
1 thread per 1000 connections". That is the design, established from source and
configuration, and not a profile of a running server. A crowded capital city
locking up one core, which is what people report, is in line with that code. In
line with is not the same as measured.

I went into this assuming that a modern core with a thread pool would scale
across cores, and that the answer to a busy realm was a bigger machine. **A tick
bound by one thread is bound by clock speed, not by core count.** And the
fastest single thread you can buy sits in a desktop socket, not a server one.
PassMark gives the Ryzen 9 9950X 4,728 on single thread against the EPYC 9965
and its 192 cores at 3,176.

Now put that process in a cluster, and the Kubernetes documentation describes
what you just agreed to:

> By default, the kubelet uses CFS quota to enforce pod CPU limits. When the
> node runs many CPU-bound pods, the workload can move to different CPU cores
> depending on whether the pod is throttled and which CPU cores are available
> at scheduling time.

That is from [CPU Management
Policies](https://kubernetes.io/docs/tasks/administer-cluster/cpu-management-policies/),
describing normal, correct behaviour. Getting exclusive cores back takes the
`static` policy, and even then only for containers that are "both part of a
`Guaranteed` pod and have integer CPU `requests`". The fix is kubelet
configuration at the machine level, and at the end of it you have a machine
dedicated to one pod with pinned cores and reserved memory. A private machine
with extra steps, under a control plane that still believes it may evict you.

The rest of the mismatch:

- **The state is in RAM, and it grows.** AzerothCore's page on memory says the
  server caches maps as players explore them and never frees them before a
  restart, ending up at 11 GB or more. They recommend starting at 16 GB
  ([memory usage](https://www.azerothcore.org/wiki/es/memory-usage), where only
  the Spanish translation is live and the English one 404s). Restarting a pod is
  restarting the server for everyone logged in.
- **There is no meaningful readiness.** A world server that has finished loading
  cannot simply replace the one that was there before. Rolling updates have
  nothing to roll over to.
- **The connection is a long-lived, raw TCP session**, not a request. Ingress
  controllers, retries and connection draining all assume the opposite.

## What belongs in the cluster

![The same system split: behind one load balancer, a cluster holding replicated auth daemons and the web front end over a shared database of accounts, characters and guilds; outside it, each world daemon on its own machine with its own read-mostly world database, reaching back into the shared database for characters and guilds.](/images/what-a-game-world-server-needs/split.svg)

Everything else, and it is more than you would think. The authentication daemon,
whose durable state lives in the database and whose process is close to
stateless. The website, the registration API, the account panel. Item shops,
armory pages, ladders and statistics, which are mostly read and can be cached.
Discord bots and status pages. Backups, migrations and content database imports,
which are exactly what Jobs and CronJobs are for. And observability for all of
it, including the world machine, which can push into the cluster without living
in it.

That list is an ordinary small platform: delivery through GitOps, a namespace
per tenant, certificates, secrets and observability, reconciled from git instead
of remembered.

The world machine wants the opposite priorities, in an order a spec sheet does
not encourage. **Clock speed first**, for the reason above. **Then RAM,
generously**, because the map cache only grows, so budget for a long uptime
rather than for the first hour. **Then local NVMe**, because the characters
database writes at every save interval and punishes a network volume. **Then
network latency, not bandwidth.** Small packets, often, where distance and
jitter decide how the game feels, and where throughput never enters the picture.

## Where the line falls

Split the system by whether a process can be killed and replaced without anyone
noticing. Everything on the yes side, meaning auth, web, shop, armory,
statistics, bots, backups and jobs, is a perfectly normal workload, and a
cluster gives it reconciled configuration, generated isolation and one place to
look when something goes wrong.

The world server stands alone on the no side. One long-lived process holding a
world in memory, driving it forward on a clock, bound by a single thread, and
talking raw TCP to clients who notice a restart within a second. It wants a fast
core, a lot of RAM, a local disk and a short path to its players. It wants,
almost exactly, [one machine](/blog/athletos-freebsd/).

The mistake is not choosing Kubernetes. The mistake is assuming the whole system
has one shape. It has two, and the cheapest architecture is the one that admits
it.

``` =html
<p class="colophon">Everything above is read rather than run: TrinityCore's source and shipped configuration, the AzerothCore and CMaNGOS documentation, the Kubernetes docs and PassMark's published ratings. The threading claim is a design established from source, not a profile of a populated server.</p>
```
